#
# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the C compliance suite (just test-c).
#
# OpenSSL 3.x allocates process-lifetime provider/method caches and a
# default certificate store the first time a TLS context is built, and
# frees them only via OPENSSL_cleanup() at true process exit. When that
# happens after LeakSanitizer's own exit-time scan, the still-reachable
# global state is reported as leaked even though nothing in this
# library — or in any well-behaved OpenSSL caller — ever loses the
# pointer. Every allocation this suppresses originates inside
# std/httpclient's cached default TLS context (getDefaultSSL), which
# jmap_client_new's built-in transport arm constructs eagerly.
#
# This suppression narrows what would otherwise be noise from a
# still-reachable OpenSSL global, but it does not make test-c a leak
# check on this library's own Nim code: Nim's allocator takes memory
# straight from mmap rather than through malloc/calloc/realloc, so
# LeakSanitizer — which instruments the malloc family — never observes
# Nim-heap allocations at all, suppressed or not. A leaked
# JmapClientHandle, or any other Nim-side allocation this library
# forgets to free, produces no LeakSanitizer report with or without
# this file. test-c's leak coverage is limited to the C side (the
# ctest harness's own allocations and anything the library hands off
# to a genuine malloc-backed C library).
leak:getDefaultSSL
